2️⃣ BINARY TREE CAMERAS (Very Popular LeetCode / Interview Problem)
📌 Problem Statement

You are given a binary tree.
A camera placed at a node can monitor:

its parent

itself

its left child

its right child

👉 Find the minimum number of cameras needed to monitor all nodes of the tree.

📌 Example
        0
       / \
      0   0
           \
            0


✅ Output: 2 cameras

💡 Key Insight (Important!)

Instead of placing cameras top-down, we decide from bottom-up.

For each node, it can be in 3 states:

State	Meaning
0	Node is not covered
1	Node has a camera
2	Node is covered (no camera)
🧠 Greedy Strategy

If any child is NOT covered → place camera here

If any child has a camera → node is covered

Else → node is not covered

⚙️ Algorithm (DFS – Post Order)

Perform post-order traversal (left → right → root)

For every node:

If left or right child state = 0 → place camera

If left or right child state = 1 → covered

Else → not covered

After traversal:

If root is not covered → add one extra camera

🧩 Java Code (Clean & Interview-Ready)
class Solution {
    int cameras = 0;

    public int minCameraCover(TreeNode root) {
        if (dfs(root) == 0) {
            cameras++;
        }
        return cameras;
    }

    // 0 = not covered
    // 1 = has camera
    // 2 = covered
    private int dfs(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return 2;

        int left = dfs(node.left);
        int right = dfs(node.right);

        if (left == 0 || right == 0) {
            cameras++;
            return 1; // has camera
        }

        if (left == 1 || right == 1) {
            return 2; // covered
        }

        return 0; // not covered
    }
}

🧪 Dry Run (Simple Case)
    0
   /
  0


Leaf → not covered

Parent sees uncovered child → place camera

Camera count = 1

⏱️ Time & Space Complexity
Type	Complexity
Time	O(N)
Space	O(H) (recursion stack)
🎯 Interview Gold Tips

Say “post-order traversal”

Mention 3-state greedy approach

Emphasize bottom-up decision

Common mistake ❌: placing cameras on leaves
